title: date_sakazuki_2_cadet
location: training grounds
---

You follow behind Fleet Admiral Sakazuki awkwardly. He walks with purpose but you aren't sure where you two are going. Isn't this just the training field? What kind of ‘unofficial work' takes place here?
{$sakazuki}: Stop, {$name}. We have come close enough.
{$name}: Eh? But the training field is over there. Isn't that where we're going?
You look around. Yeah, you are just standing in a hallway overlooking the training zone. There's nothing here. You feel even more confused than before.
{$sakazuki}: No, we are exactly where we need to be.
{$name}: Uh...
{$sakazuki}: You're confused. I can tell. No matter. Observe. The group of cadets training right now will have their finals soon.
{$sakazuki}: These are some of the last revisions they will have to go through before the exam. You went through a similar process.
{$name}: I... see.
{$sakazuki}: I want to watch over them. They are the next generation of Marines. They need to be strong enough for the challenges ahead.
{$name}: You mean like scouting high potential cadets?
{$sakazuki}: ... Something like that, but more. I want to see their promise, and their drive. If all goes well, many of them will join our forces. I will not stand for weaklings becoming a part of the Navy.
The question slips out before you can hold it back.
-> Do you watch them every day?
    {$sakazuki}: Not every day, only when I have free time.
    {$name}: But why?
    {$sakazuki}: Why not?
    {$name}: Sir... that's just answering my question with just another question.
    {$sakazuki}: Hn.
    {$sakazuki}: I have explained parts of my reasoning before. They are important. They are the next generation of Marines. I want to see their drive. I meant what I said. The second part of my reason...
    {$sakazuki}: ...
    Fleet Admiral Sakazuki doesn't say anything for a long time. His eyes focus on the cadets. Just when you think you won't get any real answer out of him...
    {$sakazuki}: <pause> They remind me of myself and my peers when we were cadets.
    {$name}: Your peers? You mean like the other Admirals?
    {$sakazuki}: And many more, a lot of them are dead, but a few are now Vice Admirals.
    -> D-Dead ones?
        <<set $sd2_points -=1>>
        {$sakazuki}: Yes, dead ones.
        {$name}: A lot of them?
        {$sakazuki}: Yes.
        {$name}: ...
        {$name}: How can you say something so morbid so calmly?! Shouldn't it freak you out more?!
        {$sakazuki}: {$name}.
        {$name}: Yes, sir?
        {$sakazuki}: Get a grip. We are Marines. Death is a part of our world.
        {$name}: I know that, but still...
        {$name}: Sorry...
        {$sakazuki}: Nevermind that. We are done here. Let's move on.
        <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Balcony>>
    -> Can you tell me a story about your cadet days?
        {$sakazuki}: ...
        You squirm under Fleet Admiral Sakazuki's sharp look.
        {$name}: Um, or not, if you aren't comfortable with it. I'm just curious.
        {$sakazuki}: They weren't any different than what you went through yourself. As I recall, the curriculum has changed very little in terms of content.
        {$name}: I don't mean it like that, I mean, cool adventures!
        {$sakazuki}: Hmm...
        {$sakazuki}: Perhaps another time. Today we have more things to get to.
        <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Balcony>>
    -> I think it's cool you watch over them. You're right. They are the future.
        <<set $sd2_points +=1>>
        Fleet Admiral Sakazuki nods curtly at your words. He watches the training for a long moment. Just when you think he is content to just stand there and observe...
        {$sakazuki}: ... I worry about what is to come. About the Navy and its future.
        {$sakazuki}: What a time to be the Fleet Admiral.
        {$name}: Sir...?
        {$sakazuki}: ... You're still here.
        {$sakazuki}: I'd appreciate it if you don't repeat what you just heard. The Navy can't be anything less than perfect.
        {$name}: U-Um, I won't, Fleet Admiral Sakazuki. Do you... want me to leave?
        {$sakazuki}: ... No. You are already here. Might as well accompany me for the rest of my tasks.
        {$sakazuki}: We are done here. Let's leave.
        <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Balcony>>
-> And what would happen if you saw someone without enough drive, sir?
    {$sakazuki}: Hmm... if they are truly not fit to be in the Navy, then they won't be in the Navy.
    {$name}: Y-You'll discharge them? Isn't that really mean?
    {$sakazuki}: Perhaps. I'm certainly not the type to hold anyone's hand.
    {$sakazuki}: They have the chance to try again if they are determined enough. But if they just want to be in the Navy for an easy ride to the top, I don't have a place for them here.
    {$sakazuki}: Do you find me too cruel?
    {$name}: You want my honest opinion? Well...
    -> There's nothing wrong with a promotion as motivation for improvement!
        <<set $sd2_points -=1>>
        That was the wrong thing to say.
        Fleet Admiral Sakazuki gives you a sharp glare.
        {$sakazuki}: There's no place in the Marines for people who seek glory over justice.
        {$name}: I mean, I wouldn't say that's seeking glory–
        {$sakazuki}: Enough. We are done here. Let's leave.
        Fleet Admiral Sakazuki marches away, leaving you behind to meekly follow after his footsteps.
        <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Balcony>>
    -> I think it's a bit too harsh... they are just cadets. Maybe they just need experience to get better.
        {$sakazuki}: Perhaps.
        {$sakazuki}: They can find such experience and perspective when they have distance from the Navy, though. If they are not ready to give this career their all, it won't work.
        {$sakazuki}: The Navy can take them back when they are ready. But until then...
        {$name}: Y-yes, Sir, I understand...
        {$sakazuki}: Hm. Let's go. I have more things to get to.
        <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Balcony>>
    -> I guess you have a point... being a Marine is hard work...
        <<set $sd2_points +=1>>
        {$sakazuki}: It is. And the standards I put out for them are high, I'm well aware. But I'd rather get people with true passion, than a bunch of weaklings without conviction.
        {$name}: I admire that. I know your... intensity scares a lot of other people though.
        {$sakazuki}: <pause>... I know.
        Fleet Admiral Sakazuki says nothing else. If he's thinking about your comment, you don't know. Finally, he gestures to you and starts walking away. You follow his lead. You can't tell if you said the right thing or not.
        <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Balcony>>
-> Did you ever oversee my training?
    {$sakazuki}: No.
    {$sakazuki}: You came from the South Blue, correct?
    {$name}: Yes, Sir.
    {$sakazuki}: It's been a while since I visited any of the Blues. The reports indicate that the training there is sufficient. I did read about your performance though. It's acceptable.
    {$name}: Thank you. That means a lot coming from you. Um...
    -> ...Maybe you can watch me train sometime? Like a date?
