title: date_sakazuki_2_Balcony
location: training grounds
---

You follow the Fleet Admiral upstairs, feeling out of place as he seemingly retrieves and hands over some documents to another officer that you aren't privy to. There are points you want to interrupt him, but there never seems to be a right time.
At last, he stops at a balcony overlooking HQ and waits for you to catch up.
{$sakazuki}: Did you get bored just walking around?
-> Absolutely not, Sir! It's my pleasure serving the Marines!
    {$sakazuki}: ... Right.
-> I have had better excursions. With that said, not everything can be fun, I guess.
    {$sakazuki}: You're right. Sometimes you need to do the things that aren't as exciting.
{$sakazuki}: Come here, {$name}. Look at New Marineford.
You follow Fleet Admiral Sakazuki's lead, looking at Headquarters from above. It's big, identical buildings and training fields sprawling far. You can see glimpses of the sea from up here too. It's beautiful.
{$name}: Wow.
{$sakazuki}: It is rather impressive, isn't it? It's a good place when you need to think. The residential area is over there... And we are standing on where the best Marines are trained and prepared.
{$name}: Huh...
-> I don't get it. It's a bunch of buildings. What's so interesting?
    <<set $sd2_points -=1>>
    {$sakazuki}:...
    {$sakazuki}: It's not just the buildings. It's the people. We are the very best the Navy has to offer. The last bastion.
    {$name}: I... don't get what you're talking about at all.
    {$sakazuki}: [annoyed/judging] Never mind that then. Come. One last errand before it's done. //HOW DO??
    <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Post_Office>>
-> I heard it's quite different from the old Marineford. Do you find it strange?
    {$sakazuki}: It was difficult to adjust at first. I do not like change. Change signifies uncertainty. And in battle, uncertainty means death.
    {$name}: But you were the one who ordered the change, right? I heard people say you ordered the move from Paradise to the New World.
    {$sakazuki}: I did.
    {$sakazuki}: It was a... calculated risk. After the Paramount War, everything was thrown into chaos yet again. A new Pirate Age, new Emperors, new forces at play...
    {$sakazuki}: It's either we wait passively for the uncertainty to consume us, or we make a move and at least we can choose what kind of surprises we face.
    {$name}: Oh... that was a tough call to make.
    {$sakazuki}: Yes. Being the Fleet Admiral isn't as glorious as it sounds.
    {$sakazuki}: It's getting late and we have one last errand to run.
    <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Post_Office>>
-> I can see why it's a nice place to think.
    <<set $sd2_points +=1>>
    {$name}: Do you mind if I come here from time to time to think?
    {$sakazuki}: I don't see why not. I don't own New Marineford.
    {$name}: ...
    {$name}: Well, I think there are people who would disagree with that, Sir. I mean, you're the Fleet Admiral.
    {$sakazuki}: Don't be foolish, {$name}. That doesn't mean I *own* Marineford. Old or new. It's called the Navy, it's not called the Fleet Admiral's Navy.
    {$name}: Well... if you don't own it... I guess the World Government does?
    {$sakazuki}: No.
    {$name}: Well... I can't think of anyone else... Maybe the people?
    {$sakazuki}: Close.
    {$sakazuki}: Justice. Justice owns us. You made a vow to serve Justice faithfully when you became a Marine, didn't you?
    {$name}: That's deep.
    {$sakazuki}: It isn't. We wear a cloak that reminds us of that vow every day.
    {$name}: ...
    {$sakazuki}: Back to your previous question. Feel free to visit here as often as you like.
    {$name}: Thank you, sir. Um, are we going somewhere?
    {$sakazuki}: Yes, one last errand before I'm done. Let's go, {$name}.
    <<jump date_sakazuki_2_Post_Office>>
