title: date_sakazuki_2_Post_Office
location: town
---

You struggle to catch up with the Fleet Admiral's long legs. Fleet Admiral Sakazuki seems to retrieve a thick stack of letters from his office before making his way to town on a path you are quite familiar with.
{$name}: Sir, are we going to the post office?
{$sakazuki}: You recognize the route?
{$name}: I send letters home often. Mom and Dad worry, so I want to reassure them I'm in good hands. And I like receiving letters from them, too.
{$sakazuki}: Hm.
{$sakazuki}: Yes. We are delivering something.
Arriving at the glassdoor of the post office, you hesitate. You aren't too sure if you should accompany the Fleet Admiral inside or not. You are curious, but it's probably private, right?
{$sakazuki}: Are you coming inside?
-> No, Sir. I prefer staying outside.
    {$sakazuki}: It's your choice. I will be back.
    You stand outside, looking in. The Fleet Admiral exchanges conversations with the post office workers before handing over the stack of paper. He meets you outside.
    {$name}: What did you send, Sir?
    {$sakazuki}: Some paperwork.
    {$name}: Oh.
    {$sakazuki}: It's done now. Thank you for your company.
    => Go to the corresponding Ending depends on the total points //HOW DO??
-> If you don't mind my presence, sir.
    <<set $sd2_points +=1>>
    {$sakazuki}: ... No. Come.
    You hesitate only for a moment before following Fleet Admiral Sakazuki inside.
    {$name}: What kind of paperwork do you need to send, Sir?
    {$sakazuki}: Hmm... death notices. I'm handing this over so they can be delivered to their respective families on different islands. Not everyone lives in New Marineford.
    {$name}: ... Oh.
    You normally try to avoid thinking about such a grim thing, but you can't help but be curious this time.
    {$name}: But why do you have to do this, Sir? Giving death notices to the post office seems... minor.
    {$sakazuki}: You are correct. Normally, I don't do such a task, but once in a while, I take over. It's a reminder to myself how big a sacrifice we have had to suffer. All these sons and daughters, never coming home except for a piece of paper.
    {$sakazuki}: If I ever faltered, it'd be like spitting on their deaths and what we stand for. We have lost so much, we must see this to the end.
    {$name}: ...
    You aren't sure what you should say to that so you keep silent, waiting for Fleet Admiral Sakazuki to finish his business.
    {$sakazuki}: It's done now. Thank you for your company.
